                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the programme`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is the annual portfolio review. The MAS intake has fallen <strong>96 → 71</strong> over three years and now contributes only <strong>~12% margin</strong>, below the <strong>18% floor</strong>. The dean's office gives you <em>one design cycle</em>. A competitor has just launched a modular “Management Certificate Stack” at <strong>30% lower cost</strong>. Read the data: separate the real demand signal (employers want applied relevance; learners want flexibility) from noise, recall that below ~60 students the fixed cost base isn't covered, and state the redesign's central tension before you touch a single lever.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the curriculum mix`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Redesign the <strong>12-module</strong> curriculum. Employers want fewer generalist modules and more applied, immediately-usable content; learners want flexibility and stackability; faculty want to protect the academic core. Rebalance toward relevance <em>without gutting the breadth that makes it a Master's</em> — convert too much and it becomes “a pile of certificates” no one will pay MAS tuition for. Build stackability deliberately so a CAS entry <em>funnels into</em> the full MAS rather than draining your best students before the high-margin later modules. Then cost the new mix against the variable-cost-per-student budget.`,
