                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_combo:         `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> The decline isn't one thing — applied relevance eroded, a cheaper stackable competitor arrived, and a price-sensitive market sits on a partly-fixed cost base. Name the combination and you can design across all of it, instead of pulling one lever and being surprised.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>“Price alone” is the trap the decision tree warns about.</strong> Cut tuition and volume may return — but your cost base barely moves, so each student contributes less and you widen the deficit. Price is a volume-and-margin trade-off, not the whole diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r1_marketing:     `<strong>“Just sell it harder” ignores why employers are walking.</strong> Two of your largest sponsors are questioning the MAS's on-the-job value while a 30%-cheaper stack courts them. No campaign sells relevance the programme doesn't have.`,
                    coach_r1_fad:           `<strong>“A temporary dip” is how a flagship gets merged away.</strong> Three years of decline and two intakes below the floor is a structural signal, not noise — wait for the market to recover and the dean's office suspends the programme first.`,
                    coach_r1_perhead:       `<strong>The per-head ratio is exactly what misleads here.</strong> Below ~60 students your fixed costs aren't covered no matter how healthy the per-student number looks. Viability is total contribution against fixed costs — volume <em>and</em> margin, together.`,
                    coach_r1_cohortignore:  `<strong>Cohort size is not a detail — it's the structural constraint.</strong> With faculty, rooms and platform largely fixed, the programme needs ~60 students just to break even on fixed costs. Ignore that and every later design choice fights a deficit.`,
                    coach_r1_employeronly:  `<strong>Designing only for employers loses the other half.</strong> Learners pay and need flexibility; if the design ignores them, the volume that fills the cohort never materialises. Continuing education has two buyers, not one.`,
                    coach_r1_marginframe:   `<strong>“Hit 18% by any means” is how you improve a ratio and break the programme.</strong> Margin is downstream of enrolment, completion and cost — chase the number alone and you'll cut your way below the viable cohort. Frame the real tension first.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the combination of causes, grounded viability in the minimum-viable-cohort logic, and framed the redesign as a two-sided problem — relevance and volume for both learner and employer, while holding margin and keeping the MAS a MAS. That's the frame the rest of the design hangs on.`,

                    coach_r2_rebalance:     `<strong>A deliberate rebalance is the modelled path.</strong> Converting about a third of the modules to applied, sector-relevant form answers the employers while a coherent generalist spine keeps the credential a Master's. Demand fit climbs because you served relevance <em>and</em> identity, not one at the cost of the other.`,
                    coach_r2_gut:           `<strong>You converted almost everything to applied content — and designed away the degree.</strong> Employers are briefly pleased, but if it's now a stack of applied certificates, why would a learner pay MAS tuition for it? You've traded the credential's identity for short-term relevance, and the two-sided value is capped.`,
                    coach_r2_freeze:        `<strong>Keeping all 12 generalist modules unchanged changes nothing employers complained about.</strong> The relevance gap that's driving the decline is still there. Protecting the academic core is right; refusing to touch <em>any</em> module is how the programme keeps sliding.`,
                    coach_r2_designed:      `<strong>That's stackability done deliberately.</strong> A CAS entry point that funnels into the full MAS widens the top of the funnel while the high-margin later modules stay protected from early exit — you grow enrolment without cannibalising the degree.`,
                    coach_r2_open:          `<strong>An open CAS exit widens the top and drains the bottom.</strong> Your best students take the certificate and leave before the high-margin later modules — you've added stackability that cannibalises the full MAS. Stackability has to be designed to funnel <em>in</em>, not leak out.`,
                    coach_r2_nostack:       `<strong>Refusing stackability protects today's revenue and ignores the trend killing you.</strong> The competitor's whole pitch is modular entry. A funnel-in CAS would widen your applicant pool — closing the door entirely cedes that ground to the stack.`,

                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>A disciplined cut — price down because cost came out.</strong> Because you took cost out of the base in Round 2, the lower tuition rebuilds volume <em>and</em> still contributes. That's the only way a price cut works: the cost structure changed with it.`,
                    coach_r3_pricecut:      `<strong>You dropped tuition but the cost base barely moved.</strong> Volume may recover, but each student now contributes less and you've widened the deficit — exactly the decision-tree warning. Discounting only works if cost comes out to match the price you cut.`,
                    coach_r3_priceraise:    `<strong>You lifted tuition to protect the per-student margin.</strong> The ratio looks healthier — but at ~8–10 fewer enrolments per 10% rise, watch you don't fall below the 60-student viable cohort, where fixed costs swallow the gain. What's your projected <em>total</em> contribution, not the per-head number?`,
                    coach_r3_sequence:      `<strong>Strong sequencing.</strong> Applied early wins build confidence and cut early drop-out; pacing the hard modules and capstone across the year lifts completion toward the 88% benchmark and smooths cash flow so revenue isn't all back-loaded.`,
                    coach_r3_seqfaculty:    `<strong>Sequencing by faculty convenience has a cost.</strong> Front-loading the hardest quantitative module spikes term-one drop-out and pushes completion down, and back-loading the revenue strains cash flow. Sequence is a completion-and-margin lever, not a logistics afterthought.`,
                    coach_r3_seqcapstone:   `<strong>Capstone first signals rigour and breaks completion.</strong> The most demanding integrative work before learners have built any footing drives early exits hardest of all. Rigour is right; front-loading it is what hurts the people you need to finish.`,
                    coach_r3_learneronly:   `<strong>Optimising only for the learner leaves the funder unanswered.</strong> Two of your largest sponsors are already questioning the MAS's value — a beautifully flexible design that never speaks to employers loses the seats they sponsor and release. Continuing education has two buyers.`,
                    coach_r3_employeronly:  `<strong>Optimising only for the employer forgets who studies.</strong> Sponsors fund the seats, but the learner lives the programme — ignore their flexibility and the part-time market simply doesn't enrol. You need both sides to fill the cohort.`,

                    coach_r4_system:        `<strong>Leading with the system is the right defence.</strong> You showed enrolment back above the viable cohort, margin above the floor, completion up and both audiences served as one coherent design — not a single ratio cherry-picked. That's the case a dean's office trusts.`,
                    coach_r4_ratio:         `<strong>Leading with the ratio invites the obvious probe.</strong> “Per-student margin looks healthy — but what's your <em>total</em> contribution, and how many students are actually enrolling?” A ratio without the volume behind it is exactly how a programme improves a number and still gets suspended.`,
                    coach_r4_identityonly:  `<strong>Arguing prestige and hoping the numbers aren't pressed is a losing hand.</strong> The dean's office has the enrolment and margin figures in front of them. Identity matters — but only alongside a viable cohort and a held margin, not instead of them.`,
                    coach_r4_differentiate: `<strong>That answers the competitor properly.</strong> A deliberately stackable MAS with employer-co-designed relevance offers what the cheaper stack can't — a credential and a funnel together. You compete on value and design, not on a price race you can't win on a fixed cost base.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>Matching a 30%-cheaper stack on price is a race you lose.</strong> Their cost base is built for it; yours isn't. Undercut them and you drop below the contribution you need — the answer to a cheaper competitor is differentiation, not a price war.`,
                    coach_r4_compignore:    `<strong>Ignoring the stack doesn't make it go away.</strong> It's already courting two of your sponsors. “The MAS brand is above competing” is precisely the complacency that let intake slide from 96 to 71. Answer the threat with design.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>Web traffic and downloads are interest, not intent.</strong> They won't tell you in June whether September will fill. Watch the signals that convert — applications, deposits and sponsorship commitments — or you'll learn the redesign failed only when the cohort doesn't show.`,
                    coach_r4_noind:         `<strong>Waiting for the September number is waiting too long.</strong> By then the design is locked and the dean's decision is made. Leading indicators — applications, early deposits, employer commitments — are the early-warning system this redesign needs.`,

