                    insight_restored:       `Programme restored: you ran the genuine redesign — a deliberate curriculum rebalance that kept the MAS a Master's, funnel-in stackability, disciplined pricing matched to a leaner cost base, and a sequence that lifted completion. Enrolment is back above the viable cohort, margin is above the 18% floor, and the design serves both the paying learner and the sponsoring employer. The flagship survives as a distinct programme.`,
                    insight_priceonly:      `Price-only margin fix: you lifted tuition hard to repair the per-student ratio, and elasticity priced the cohort away — even where you scraped back to ~60 students it was the price rise, not the design, doing the work, and any further raise tips below the viable cohort where fixed costs swallow the gain. You improved a ratio at the expense of the volume the programme runs on. Margin is total contribution — volume and per-student contribution together — not a number to hit by pricing students out.`,
                    insight_discount:       `Volume-only discounting: you cut tuition to chase students without taking matching cost out of the base, so each student contributes less and the deficit widened. A price cut only works when the cost structure changes with it.`,
                    insight_identityloss:   `Identity loss: you stripped the generalist core to please employers until the credential no longer reads as a Master's. Relevant in the short term — but if it's a pile of applied certificates, no learner pays MAS tuition for it, and the two-sided value is capped.`,
                    insight_onesided:       `One-sided design: you optimised for a single buyer — learner flexibility or employer relevance — and lost the other. Continuing education has two buyers who fill the cohort together; serve only one and the enrolment leaks from the side you ignored.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed redesign: some of the levers landed, but the mix, price, sequence and defence didn't fully reinforce each other — enough seams that the dean's office still questions whether the September intake is safe.`,
                    insight_cohort:         `You finished below the minimum viable cohort of ~60. With a partly-fixed cost base, that is a structural deficit no pricing or content choice can rescue — the programme cannot cover its fixed costs at this volume.`,
                    insight_margin_low:     `Margin finished below the 18% floor. The dean's office signalled a programme cannot run below margin indefinitely — another intake here and the MAS is suspended or merged.`,
                    insight_completion:     `Completion stayed below the 88% benchmark — the sequence let drop-out time and pacing erode the cohort, costing both credential value and the contribution of the students who left.`,
                    insight_cannibal:       `Stackability cannibalised the full MAS: an open exit let your best students take a certificate and leave before the high-margin later modules. Stackability has to funnel learners in, not leak them out.`,
